                <Attribute Type="public definition">PTEN hamartoma tumor syndrome (PHTS) is characterized by skin and mucosal lesions, macrocephaly, gastrointestinal polyps, and a high risk for benign and malignant tumors of the breast, thyroid, and endometrium. There is also an increased risk of renal cell carcinoma, colorectal carcinoma, and melanoma. Some individuals have developmental delay and/or autism spectrum disorder. PHTS includes the following clinical phenotypes: Cowden syndrome (CS), Bannayan-Riley-Ruvalcaba syndrome (BRRS), Proteus-like syndrome, and apparently isolated adult-onset Lhermitte-Duclos disease (LDD). Regardless of clinical phenotype, individuals with a germline PTEN pathogenic variant are thought to have the same cancer risks.</Attribute>
